# Agents at scale

The thing a "swarm" really is: decompose a task into many pieces, run an agent on each
piece *at the same time*, then synthesize the results. The hard part is usually the
plumbing — a job queue, workers, a result bus. On the [Nexus](/introduction/what-is-the-nexus)
that's the BEAM's day job, so the orchestration is a few lines in a `server` block.

Here is the whole fan-out — plan, dispatch a worker per sub-task concurrently, synthesize:

```
  server :swarm do
    def run(task) do
      task
      |> plan()                                   # 1 LLM call → N focused sub-tasks
      |> Enum.map(fn q -> Task.async(fn -> worker(q) end) end)
      |> Task.await_many(:infinity)               # all agents run in parallel
      |> synthesize(task)                          # fold findings into one answer
    end

    defp plan(task) do
      {:ok, %{content: c}} =
        Nexus.Llm.complete([%{role: "user",
          content: "Break this into distinct sub-questions, one per line:\n#{task}"}], [])
      c |> String.split("\n") |> Enum.reject(&(&1 == ""))
    end

    defp worker(q) do
      {:ok, page} = Nexus.Browse.read(top_result(q))   # the built-in browser
      {:ok, %{content: finding}} =
        Nexus.Llm.complete([%{role: "user",
          content: "Answer from this page only:\n#{q}\n\n#{page.text}"}], [])
      %{q: q, finding: finding}
    end
  end
```

`Task.async` over a list and `Task.await_many` is the entire concurrency model — five
agents or five hundred is the same code, only the list is longer. Each `worker` pulls a
real page with the built-in browser and grounds its answer in what it read; the runtime
accounts the true LLM cost on every call.

## Why it stays small

- **No queue, no workers, no bus** — `Task.async` + the BEAM *is* the scheduler.
- **Real tools, brokered** — `Nexus.Llm.complete` and `Nexus.Browse.read` are host
  primitives; the agent code just calls them.
- **One server unit** holds the whole orchestration — the same shape powers our own
  search swarm, distilled here to its core.

This is the pattern the [coding agent](/examples/coding-agent) and
[autonomous research](/examples/autonomous-research) examples fan out with — swap the
`worker` for whatever the job is.
